tel 1.0

base pneumatic
import soundness

info   compression formats as types: DEFLATE, gzip, zlib, Brotli, XZ, LZMA2 and LZW, over values and streams
detail
    _Pneumatic_ compresses and decompresses bytes through one pair of verbs, `compress` and
    `decompress`, for every format it knows. A format is a **type** — `Gzip`, `Zlib`,
    `Deflate`, `Brotli`, `Xz`, `Lzma2` or `Lzw` — and the verb takes it as a type argument,
    so the format is checked where the code compiles rather than parsed from a string or a
    flag at runtime. Each format's companion holds a `Compression` given, which is the
    typeclass behind the verbs, and nothing else about a codec need be constructed, fed or
    finished by hand.

    The same verbs work on a whole `Data` value, on a lazy `Chain[Data]` of chunks, and on a
    streaming `Stream[Data] over Credit`, where they become pipeline stages: the bytes are
    transformed as they flow, and nothing larger than the pipeline's buffers is held (except
    by the formats whose algorithm needs the whole value, which `Brotli` does). All three forms
    are driven through one `Duct` per format, so they agree byte for byte and interoperate
    freely: a value compressed as a whole decompresses as a stream, and a stream's output
    decompresses as a value.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import charsets.utf8Charset
    import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val payload: Data = t"the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog".in[Data]
    val compressed: Data = payload.compress[Gzip]
    compressed.decompress[Gzip].utf8   // t"the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"

    payload.compress[Xz].decompress[Xz] == payload   // true
    ```

    The pipeline form puts the compressor and decompressor on either side of a file, a socket
    or an HTTP body. Here a stream grows one stage at a time, from reading the bytes to
    writing them compressed:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label The bytes, as a stream of chunks
      code
          val source: Stream[Data] over Credit = payload.stream
    frame
      label Compressed as they flow
      code
          val source: Stream[Data] over Credit = payload.stream.compress[Gzip]
    frame
      label Decompressed again, downstream of the compressor
      code
          val source: Stream[Data] over Credit = payload.stream.compress[Gzip].decompress[Gzip]
    frame
      label Read back as text at the end of the pipeline
      code
          val text: Text = payload.stream.compress[Gzip].decompress[Gzip].read[Text]
    ##
    ```

    Every pipeline stage takes a `Buffering` contextual value, which sizes the duct's buffers;
    `Buffering.standard` is in implicit scope, so no import is needed unless another policy is
    wanted.

    The formats, and what each is for:

    - `Deflate` is the raw DEFLATE bit stream, `Zlib` the same stream in its two-byte header and
      Adler-32 trailer, and `Gzip` the same stream in the header and CRC-32 trailer of a `.gz`
      file. They are the formats of HTTP content encoding, ZIP entries and PNG chunks, and the
      fastest of the set. `gzip` and `gunzip` are one-shot extensions on `Data` for the
      commonest of them.
    - `Brotli` compresses smaller than DEFLATE at comparable speed, and is the modern web's
      content encoding. `Brotli.prefix` and `Brotli.continuation` encode a value relative to a
      base the receiver already holds.
    - `Xz` is the high-ratio codec, LZMA2 in the `.xz` container with a CRC-64 check, matching
      the `xz` command-line tool; `Lzma2` is the same codec without the container, as `Deflate`
      is to `Gzip`. Both take a preset from 0 (fastest) to 9 (smallest), defaulting to 6.
    - `Lzw` is the compression of TIFF and PDF streams, which the JDK does not implement.

    Corrupt or truncated input is reported by the decoders as a thrown `IllegalStateException`
    whose message begins _the compressed data is corrupt_, _the XZ data is corrupt_, _the
    LZMA2 data is corrupt_ or _the Brotli data is corrupt_; there is no `Error` type to handle
    through a `Tactic`, so a decoder reading untrusted input is wrapped in a `safely` or a
    `try` at the call site.

    The library is one package in several components, so a consumer of one format need not
    compile the others:

    - `pneumatic.core` — `Compression`, `Compressor`, the `compress`/`decompress` extensions,
      and the pure-Scala DEFLATE engines ported from JZlib. Every platform.
    - `pneumatic.flate` — `Deflate`, `Gzip`, `Zlib`, `gzip` and `gunzip`. It is compiled twice:
      on the JVM over `java.util.zip` (native zlib and its CRC-32 intrinsics), and for Scala.js,
      Scala Native and WASI over the pure-Scala engines in `core`. The output of the two is
      byte-identical, and the pure engines are exercised by the JVM test suite as well.
    - `pneumatic.brotli` — `Brotli`, with its 122 KB static dictionary.
    - `pneumatic.lzma` — `Xz` and `Lzma2`.
    - `pneumatic.lzw` — `Lzw`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, so `import soundness.*` brings in the
    formats and the verbs, and every `Compression` given resolves through its format's
    companion without an import.

refer pneumatic.Compression
refer pneumatic.compress
refer pneumatic.decompress
refer pneumatic.Gzip
refer pneumatic.Brotli
refer pneumatic.Xz

entry .Compression   the typeclass a compression format implements: a compressor and a decompressor duct
  detail
      `format is Compression` is what `compress[format]` and `decompress[format]` summon. A
      format is a type extending `Compressor`, and its companion holds the given: `Gzip.compression`,
      `Zlib.compression`, `Deflate.compression`, `Brotli.compression`, `Xz.compression`,
      `Lzma2.compression` and `Lzw.compression`, each in implicit scope through the companion.

      The typeclass has two abstract members, `#compressor` and `#decompressor`, each a factory
      for a fresh `Duct[Data, Data]` stage; the `Chain` forms `#compress` and `#decompress` are
      derived from them by driving the chunks through the same duct, which is how the
      whole-value, chain and stream forms are kept byte-for-byte identical. A format with a
      cheaper chunk-by-chunk driver (`Xz`, `Lzma2`, `Lzw`, `Brotli`) overrides the two derived
      members, but the contract is the same.

      A new format is a sealed trait and a companion given:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      sealed trait Identity extends Compressor

      object Identity:
        given compression: Identity is Compression:
          def compressor()(using Buffering): (Duct[Data, Data] {
            type Transport = Credit
            type Upstream = Credit })^ =
            Deflate.compression.compressor()

          def decompressor()(using Buffering): (Duct[Data, Data] {
            type Transport = Credit
            type Upstream = Credit })^ =
            Deflate.compression.decompressor()
      ```

      The same bytes, under each format in turn:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The fastest: raw DEFLATE
        code
            payload.compress[Deflate].length
      frame
        label In the gzip framing a `.gz` file carries
        code
            payload.compress[Gzip].length   // ten bytes of header and eight of trailer more
      frame
        label Brotli: smaller, at a similar speed
        code
            payload.compress[Brotli].length
      frame
        label XZ: the smallest, at the highest cost
        code
            payload.compress[Xz].length
      ##
      ```
  refer pneumatic.Compressor
  refer pneumatic.compress
  refer pneumatic.decompress
  entry #Self   the format this instance serves, a subtype of `Compressor`
  entry #compress   a lazy chain of chunks, compressed chunk by chunk
      `compression.compress(chain)` is what `chain.compress[format]` calls. The default
      implementation adapts the chain to a stream, runs it through `#compressor`, and yields one
      output chunk per refill of the duct; the result is a lazy `Chain[Data]`, so nothing is
      compressed until it is consumed. The chunk boundaries of the output bear no relation to
      those of the input.
  entry #decompress   a lazy chain of compressed chunks, decompressed chunk by chunk
      `compression.decompress(chain)` is what `chain.decompress[format]` calls, through
      `#decompressor`. Input may be split at any byte boundary: a gzip header or an LZMA2 chunk
      header fed one byte at a time is reassembled by the stage. Corrupt input throws
      `IllegalStateException` when the chain is forced, not when it is built.
  entry #compressor   a fresh compressing `Duct[Data, Data]` stage, for a streaming pipeline
      The factory `stream.compress[format]` and `data.compress[format]` use. It takes a
      `Buffering` and returns a duct whose `Transport` and `Upstream` are both `Credit`: demand
      passes through unchanged, since a compression ratio cannot be known ahead, and the duct's
      own bounded buffer absorbs expansion. Each call makes a new duct with its own state, so
      a duct is never shared between two streams.
  entry #decompressor   a fresh decompressing `Duct[Data, Data]` stage, for a streaming pipeline
      The factory `stream.decompress[format]` and `data.decompress[format]` use, taking a
      `Buffering`. The DEFLATE family stops at the end of its stream and consumes a gzip
      trailer without verifying it; `Xz` verifies the CRC-32, CRC-64 or SHA-256 check of every
      block, and a truncated stream is an error.

entry .Compressor   the marker type every compression format extends
    `Deflate`, `Gzip`, `Zlib`, `Brotli`, `Xz`, `Lzma2` and `Lzw` are each a `sealed trait
    extends Compressor`, with no instances: a format is named only as a type argument, as in
    `compress[Gzip]`, and the bound `format <: Compressor` on the verbs keeps an unrelated type
    out of that position. The format's behaviour lives entirely in its `Compression` given.

entry .compress   `value.compress[Format]`: compresses a value, a chain of chunks or a stream
  detail
      Three extension methods share the name, chosen by the receiver:

      - on a `Data`, the whole value is compressed through the format's duct and returned as a
        `Data`;
      - on a `Chain[Data]`, a lazy chain of chunks is returned, compressed as it is forced;
      - on a `Stream[Data] over Credit`, the format's compressor becomes a stage of the
        pipeline, and the result is another `Stream[Data] over Credit`. The receiver is
        consumed: it may not be read again after the stage is attached.

      Each needs a `format is Compression`, found in the format's companion, and the `Data` and
      `Stream` forms a `Buffering`, for which `Buffering.standard` is in implicit scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = Data(1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34)
      val whole: Data = payload.compress[Gzip]
      val chunks: Chain[Data] = Chain(payload).compress[Gzip]
      val stream: Stream[Data] over Credit = payload.stream.compress[Gzip]

      chunks.flat.to[List] == whole.to[List]   // true: the forms agree byte for byte
      ```

      An explicit XZ or LZMA2 preset, or an LZW `earlyChange` setting, is not expressible
      through the verb: `Xz.compress(chain, preset)`, `Xz.compressor(preset)`,
      `Lzma2.compress(chain, preset)` and `Lzw.compress(chain, earlyChange)` take the
      parameter directly.
  refer pneumatic.decompress
  refer pneumatic.Compression

entry .decompress   `value.decompress[Format]`: decompresses a value, a chain of chunks or a stream
  detail
      The inverse of `compress`, with the same three receivers: a `Data` decompresses to a
      `Data`, a `Chain[Data]` to a lazy `Chain[Data]`, and a `Stream[Data] over Credit` gains
      the format's decompressor as a stage, consuming the receiver. Each needs a `format is
      Compression` from the format's companion, and the `Data` and `Stream` forms a `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = Data(1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34)
      payload.compress[Brotli].decompress[Brotli] == payload   // true
      payload.compress[Brotli].stream.decompress[Brotli].read[Data] == payload
      ```

      The forms interoperate: what `compress` produced as a whole decompresses through a
      stream, and the other way round. Input which is not in the named format, is corrupt, or
      is truncated throws an `IllegalStateException` — when the `Data` form returns, or when
      the chain or stream is forced — with a message naming the format and the fault; a
      decoder over untrusted bytes is wrapped in `safely` or a `try`. Raw `Lzma2` carries no
      dictionary size, so `decompress[Lzma2]` assumes the preset-6 dictionary of 8 MiB;
      `Lzma2.decompress(chain, dictSize)` takes another.
  refer pneumatic.compress
  refer pneumatic.Compression

entry .Deflate   the raw DEFLATE stream, with no header or checksum
  detail
      `Deflate` names the bare DEFLATE bit stream of RFC 1951: the compressed blocks alone,
      with none of the framing `Zlib` and `Gzip` put around them. It is the form ZIP entries,
      PNG chunks and HTTP's `deflate` content encoding (in practice) carry, and the one to use
      when the container supplies its own length and checksum.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = Data(1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34)
      payload.compress[Deflate].decompress[Deflate] == payload   // true
      ```

      The deflater has a single compression level, zlib's default of 6: there is no parameter
      to choose another. On the JVM the stream is produced by `java.util.zip`; elsewhere by
      the pure-Scala port in `pneumatic.core`, with byte-identical output. A corrupt stream
      throws `IllegalStateException`, _the compressed data is corrupt: …_, as it is read.
  refer pneumatic.Zlib
  refer pneumatic.Gzip
  entry .compression   the `Deflate is Compression` given, in implicit scope through the companion
      Its compressor is a raw deflater and its decompressor a raw inflater, each a
      `Duct[Data, Data]` over `Credit`, built fresh for every stream and needing a `Buffering`.

entry .Gzip   DEFLATE in the framing of a `.gz` file: header, CRC-32 and length trailer
  detail
      `Gzip` is the format of `.gz` files, of `tar.gz` archives and of HTTP's `gzip` content
      encoding (RFC 1952): a ten-byte header, a raw DEFLATE stream, then the CRC-32 and length
      of the original bytes. Compressing writes the minimal header, with no name, comment or
      timestamp; decompressing accepts any header, including the optional extra, name, comment
      and header-checksum fields, consumes the trailer and does not verify it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = t"the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog".in[Data]
      payload.compress[Gzip].decompress[Gzip].utf8   // t"the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"
      payload.stream.compress[Gzip].decompress[Gzip].read[Text]
      ```

      The stream decompressor reassembles a header or trailer however the input is chunked, so
      a gzip stream arriving a byte at a time decodes. `gzip` and `gunzip` are the one-shot
      extensions on `Data` for the same transformation. On the JVM the DEFLATE stream and the
      CRC-32 come from `java.util.zip`; elsewhere from the pure-Scala port, byte-identically.
  refer pneumatic.gzip
  refer pneumatic.gunzip
  refer pneumatic.Deflate
  entry .compression   the `Gzip is Compression` given, in implicit scope through the companion
      Its compressor writes the header, the raw DEFLATE stream and the CRC-32 and size trailer;
      its decompressor parses the header with a small state machine ahead of the inflater and
      skips the eight-byte trailer after it finishes. Each is a `Duct[Data, Data]` over
      `Credit` needing a `Buffering`.

entry .Zlib   DEFLATE in the zlib wrapper: a two-byte header and an Adler-32 trailer
  detail
      `Zlib` is the format of RFC 1950, the deflater's own framing: two bytes declaring the
      method and window, the DEFLATE stream, and an Adler-32 checksum of the original bytes. It
      is the compression inside PNG's `IDAT` chunks, of git's loose objects and packfile
      entries, and of PDF's `FlateDecode` streams.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = Data(1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34)
      payload.compress[Zlib].decompress[Zlib] == payload   // true
      ```

      Besides the `Compression` given, `.inflate` decodes one zlib stream which begins at an
      offset in a larger value and reports how many bytes it consumed, for the containers
      which concatenate zlib streams without framing them.
  refer pneumatic.Deflate
  refer pneumatic.Gzip
  entry .compression   the `Zlib is Compression` given, in implicit scope through the companion
      Its compressor and decompressor are the deflater and inflater with the zlib wrapper
      enabled, each a `Duct[Data, Data]` over `Credit` needing a `Buffering`.
  entry .inflate   inflates the one zlib stream starting at an offset, with the count of bytes it consumed
      `Zlib.inflate(data, offset)` decodes the zlib stream which begins at `offset` in `data`
      and returns a pair: the decompressed bytes, and the number of compressed bytes the stream
      occupied, so the next thing in `data` can be found. This is the shape git's packfiles
      have — object after object, each a zlib stream, with no length recorded between them —
      and is what a packfile reader needs where `decompress[Zlib]` would decode the first
      stream and discard the rest.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val first: Data = Data(1, 2, 3).compress[Zlib]
      val second: Data = Data(4, 5, 6).compress[Zlib]
      val packed: Data = Chain(first, second).flat.to[Array]

      val (one, consumed) = Zlib.inflate(packed, 0)
      val (two, _) = Zlib.inflate(packed, consumed)
      one.to[List] == List[Byte](1, 2, 3) && two.to[List] == List[Byte](4, 5, 6)
      ```

      A stream which ends before its data is complete throws `IllegalStateException`, _the
      compressed data is truncated_.

entry .gzip   `data.gzip`: a whole value compressed as gzip, in one call
    An extension on `Data` which compresses the whole value in the `Gzip` format and returns
    the result as one `Data`; `data.compress[Gzip]` is the same transformation through the
    duct. It needs nothing in scope. On the JVM it runs over `java.util.zip`, elsewhere over
    the pure-Scala deflater, so it is available on every platform; `bitumen` uses it to gzip a
    whole tar archive.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val payload: Data = Data(1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34)
    payload.gzip.gunzip == payload   // true
    ```

entry .gunzip   `data.gunzip`: a whole gzip value decompressed, in one call
    An extension on `Data` which decompresses a whole `Gzip` value and returns the original
    bytes as one `Data`, with no contextual values needed; `data.decompress[Gzip]` is the same
    transformation through the duct. Input which is not gzip throws `IllegalStateException`.

entry .Xz   LZMA2 in the `.xz` container, with a CRC-64 check: what the `xz` tool produces
  detail
      `Xz` is the high-ratio codec: the LZMA2 algorithm inside the `.xz` container of XZ Utils,
      as a pure-Scala port of the public-domain algorithm, so it runs on every platform. A
      value compressed with the default preset decodes with the `xz` command-line tool and the
      tool's output decodes here, including streams it wrote with a CRC-32, SHA-256 or no
      check; the check of every block is verified on decoding, so a corrupted payload is
      detected. The compressor writes a CRC-64 check, as `xz` does by default.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      payload.compress[Xz].decompress[Xz] == payload   // true
      payload.compress[Xz].length < payload.length     // true
      ```

      The preset, 0 to 9, trades speed for ratio: 0 to 3 use a fast greedy encoder and 4 to 9
      a price-based one which weighs every candidate match, and a higher preset also means a
      larger dictionary, from 256 KiB at preset 0 to 64 MiB at preset 9. `compress[Xz]` uses
      `.DefaultPreset`, 6; `.compress` and `.compressor` take a preset of the caller's choice.
      The encoder is streaming and bounded in memory: it buffers at most one dictionary's worth
      of input, emits each as a self-contained block, and closes the stream with its index and
      footer, so a long input becomes a multi-block `.xz` stream. The decoder is a streaming
      state machine too, and decompresses a 4 MB payload at about the speed of the reference
      Java implementation.

      `Lzma2` is the same codec without the container framing, standing to `Xz` as `Deflate`
      stands to `Gzip`. Corrupt input throws `IllegalStateException`: _the data is not in XZ
      format: bad magic bytes_ for something which is not `.xz` at all, _the XZ data is
      corrupt: integrity check failed_ for a check mismatch, and _the XZ data is corrupt:
      truncated_ for a stream which ends early. A stream using a filter other than LZMA2 (the
      BCJ branch filters, delta) throws _this XZ stream uses an unsupported filter_.
  refer pneumatic.Lzma2
  refer pneumatic.Compression
  entry .DefaultPreset   the preset `compress[Xz]` uses: 6, the `xz` tool's default
      An `inline val` of 6: an 8 MiB dictionary and the price-based encoder, within a few
      percent of the `xz` tool's ratio on text and ahead of it on repetitive data.
  entry .compression   the `Xz is Compression` given, in implicit scope through the companion
      Its compressor encodes at `.DefaultPreset` with a CRC-64 check, and its decompressor
      accepts any check type and verifies it. The `Chain` forms drive an engine chunk by chunk
      rather than through the duct, with the same output.
  entry .compressor   a compressing duct at an explicit preset, 0 to 9
      `Xz.compressor(preset)` is the streaming stage `compress[Xz]` would use, at the chosen
      preset instead of 6, for a pipeline built with `viaDuct`. It needs a `Buffering`. A preset
      outside 0 to 9 is clamped.
  entry .compress   compresses a chain of chunks at an explicit preset, 0 to 9
      `Xz.compress(chain, preset)` is `chain.compress[Xz]` at the chosen preset: a lazy
      `Chain[Data]` of `.xz` bytes, which decodes with `decompress[Xz]`, since the container
      records everything the decoder needs.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      Xz.compress(Chain(payload), 1).decompress[Xz].flat.to[List] == payload.to[List]
      ```
  entry .decompress   decompresses a chain of `.xz` chunks
      `Xz.decompress(chain)` is `chain.decompress[Xz]` as a method on the companion, for code
      which holds the format as a value rather than a type argument. The check type and
      dictionary size are read from the stream.

entry .Lzma2   raw LZMA2 with no container: the codec inside `.xz`, as `Deflate` is to `Gzip`
  detail
      `Lzma2` is the chunked LZMA stream which `.xz` carries, without the container's magic
      bytes, block headers, index and checks: each chunk declares its own uncompressed and
      compressed sizes and whether the dictionary, state and properties reset at its start,
      and a control byte of zero ends the stream. It is the form to use inside a container of
      one's own, or for a format (such as 7z) which frames LZMA2 itself.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      payload.compress[Lzma2].decompress[Lzma2] == payload   // true
      ```

      What the container would have recorded, the caller must know: a raw LZMA2 stream does
      not say which dictionary size it was encoded with, so `decompress[Lzma2]` assumes the
      dictionary of `.DefaultPreset` (8 MiB), and a stream encoded at another preset with
      `.compress` or `.compressor` is decoded with `.decompress` or `.decompressor` given the
      dictionary size that preset used: 256 KiB, 1 MiB, 2 MiB, 4 MiB, 4 MiB, 8 MiB, 8 MiB,
      16 MiB, 32 MiB and 64 MiB for presets 0 to 9. A size too small for the stream's
      backward references fails as corrupt, _the LZMA data is corrupt: invalid match distance_.

      Unlike `Xz`, the raw encoder buffers its whole input before encoding, since there is no
      block framing to segment it by; the decoder is incremental and holds no more than one
      chunk's compressed payload. Corrupt input throws `IllegalStateException` with a message
      beginning _the LZMA2 data is corrupt_.
  refer pneumatic.Xz
  entry .UncompressedSizeMax   the most uncompressed bytes one LZMA2 chunk may carry: 2 MiB
  entry .CompressedSizeMax   the most compressed bytes one LZMA chunk may carry: 64 KiB
  entry .DefaultPreset   the preset `compress[Lzma2]` uses, and whose 8 MiB dictionary `decompress[Lzma2]` assumes: 6
  entry .compression   the `Lzma2 is Compression` given, in implicit scope through the companion
      Its compressor encodes at `.DefaultPreset` and its decompressor assumes that preset's
      8 MiB dictionary. The `Chain` forms drive an engine chunk by chunk rather than through the
      duct, with the same output.
  entry .compressor   a compressing duct at an explicit preset, 0 to 9
      `Lzma2.compressor(preset)` is the streaming stage `compress[Lzma2]` would use, at the
      chosen preset, for a pipeline built with `viaDuct`; it needs a `Buffering`. Its output
      must be decoded with the dictionary size of that preset.
  entry .compress   compresses a chain of chunks at an explicit preset, 0 to 9
      `Lzma2.compress(chain, preset)`: a lazy `Chain[Data]` of raw LZMA2, to be decoded with
      `.decompress(chain, dictSize)` given the preset's dictionary size, which the stream does
      not record.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      val encoded: Chain[Data] = Lzma2.compress(Chain(payload), 1)
      Lzma2.decompress(encoded, 1 << 20).flat.to[List] == payload.to[List]   // preset 1: 1 MiB
      ```
  entry .decompressor   a decompressing duct for a stream encoded with a known dictionary size
      `Lzma2.decompressor(dictSize)` is the streaming stage `decompress[Lzma2]` would use,
      with the dictionary size in bytes the producer used instead of the assumed 8 MiB; it
      needs a `Buffering`.
  entry .decompress   decompresses a chain of raw LZMA2 chunks with a known dictionary size
      `Lzma2.decompress(chain, dictSize)` decodes a stream produced at a preset other than 6,
      given that preset's dictionary size in bytes; `chain.decompress[Lzma2]` is the same call
      with 8 MiB.

entry .Lzw   the LZW compression of TIFF and PDF streams, written from the specification
  detail
      `Lzw` is Lempel-Ziv-Welch as TIFF's compression scheme 5 and PDF's `LZWDecode` filter
      define it: 8-bit input symbols, MSB-first codes starting at 9 bits and widening to 12, a clear code
      of 256 and an end-of-information code of 257. The JDK has no implementation of it, so
      this one is written from the specification and runs on every platform.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      payload.compress[Lzw].decompress[Lzw] == payload   // true
      ```

      The one parameter is `earlyChange`: whether both sides widen their codes one table entry
      sooner than the arithmetic strictly requires. It is on in `compress[Lzw]`, since it is
      what TIFF, PDF and every known encoder produce; a format which states the other setting
      (PDF's `EarlyChange 0`) uses `.compress`, `.decompress`, `.compressor` or
      `.decompressor` with it off. Both sides must agree, or the output is garbage with no
      error raised.
  refer pneumatic.Compression
  entry .compression   the `Lzw is Compression` given, with `earlyChange` on, in implicit scope through the companion
      The TIFF and PDF default. Its compressor and decompressor are `Duct[Data, Data]` stages
      over `Credit` needing a `Buffering`; the `Chain` forms drive the engine chunk by chunk.
  entry .compressor   a compressing duct with `earlyChange` chosen explicitly
      `Lzw.compressor(earlyChange)` is the streaming stage for a pipeline built with
      `viaDuct`; it needs a `Buffering`. `compress[Lzw]` is the same with `earlyChange = true`.
  entry .decompressor   a decompressing duct with `earlyChange` chosen explicitly
      `Lzw.decompressor(earlyChange)` is the streaming stage for a pipeline built with
      `viaDuct`; it needs a `Buffering`. `decompress[Lzw]` is the same with `earlyChange = true`.
  entry .compress   compresses a chain of chunks, with `earlyChange` on unless given as `false`
      `Lzw.compress(chain, earlyChange = true)`: a lazy `Chain[Data]`, each input chunk's
      codes emitted as it is consumed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      val late: Chain[Data] = Lzw.compress(Chain(payload), earlyChange = false)
      Lzw.decompress(late, earlyChange = false).flat.to[List] == payload.to[List]
      ```
  entry .decompress   decompresses a chain of chunks, with `earlyChange` on unless given as `false`
      `Lzw.decompress(chain, earlyChange = true)`: the inverse of `.compress` at the same
      setting, as a lazy `Chain[Data]`.

entry .Brotli   the Brotli format of RFC 7932: the web's content encoding, with relative encoding against a base
  detail
      `Brotli` compresses smaller than DEFLATE at a comparable speed, and is the `br` content
      encoding of HTTP. The decoder is a faithful port of Google's reference decoder, with the
      format's 122 KB static dictionary, so it decodes anything a browser or server emits; the
      encoder is a fast, specification-compliant path. Both are pure Scala, on every platform.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val payload: Data = (t"the quick brown fox " * 40).in[Data]
      payload.compress[Brotli].decompress[Brotli] == payload   // true
      payload.compress[Brotli].length < payload.compress[Gzip].length
      ```

      Both directions need the whole value before producing any output — the decoder because
      a backward reference may reach across the entire window, the encoder because it chooses
      its framing from the total length — so a Brotli stage in a pipeline buffers everything
      it is given and emits on flush, where a DEFLATE stage emits as it goes. It is still the
      same stage, and interoperates with the whole-value form.

      Brotli can also encode a value *relative to* a base the receiver already holds, which is
      how a successor differing from its predecessor by a few edits costs a few bytes.
      `.continuation` encodes the new value against a window preloaded with the base, as the
      headerless tail of a stream; `.prefix` is the priming stream which puts the base in a
      decoder's window, whose bytes are fixed by the format and two parameters alone, so both
      sides build it without transmitting it. Decoding the prefix and the continuation as one
      stream yields the base followed by the successor.

      Corrupt input throws `IllegalStateException` with a message beginning _the Brotli data
      is corrupt_.
  refer pneumatic.Compression
  refer pneumatic.Gzip
  entry .Window   the largest window Brotli allows, as WBITS: 24, the default for `.prefix` and `.continuation`
      A window of `2^24 - 16` bytes. The prefix is never transmitted, so the widest window
      costs nothing and only widens what a continuation may reference.
  entry .Block   the largest meta-block Brotli allows: `1 << 24` bytes, the default for `.prefix`
  entry .continuation   encodes a value against a window already holding a base
      `Brotli.continuation(base, next, window = Brotli.Window)` is the meta-block(s) for `next`
      alone, with no stream header, byte-aligned and ending with the last-block flag, whose
      backward references may reach up to `2^window - 16` bytes back, into `base`. It is a
      valid tail of any stream which leaves `base` in the decoder's window, and in particular
      of `.prefix(base, window, block)`. `window` is WBITS, 10 to 24, and must be the value
      the receiver's prefix declares. An empty `next` gives the empty last meta-block.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val base: Data = t"the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog".in[Data]
      val next: Data = t"the quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog".in[Data]
      val continuation: Data = Brotli.continuation(base, next)
      val decoded: Data = Chain(Brotli.prefix(base), continuation).decompress[Brotli].read[Data]
      decoded.length == base.length + next.length   // true: the base, then the successor
      ```

      A continuation decoded against the wrong base decodes without error to the wrong bytes:
      nothing in the format identifies the base, so the sender and receiver must agree on it
      by other means. A successor which does not compress against its base falls back to
      stored meta-blocks.
  entry .prefix   the priming stream which leaves a base in a decoder's window
      `Brotli.prefix(base, window = Brotli.Window, block = Brotli.Block)` is a stream header
      declaring `window` (WBITS, 10 to 24), then `base` as uncompressed meta-blocks of at most
      `block` bytes (1 to `2^24`), then the empty metadata meta-block, the byte `0x06`. Its
      bytes are determined by RFC 7932 and the three arguments alone, so a receiver holding
      `base` builds the identical prefix locally. It is not a complete stream — it awaits a
      `.continuation` — so decoding it alone fails; an empty base primes to two bytes, and a
      base of 4,096 bytes to `3 + 4096 + 1` by default.
  entry .compression   the `Brotli is Compression` given, in implicit scope through the companion
      Its compressor and decompressor are buffering `Duct[Data, Data]` stages over `Credit`,
      needing a `Buffering`, which accumulate the whole input and produce their output on
      flush. The `Chain` forms feed the engine every chunk and yield the result as one chunk.
